  :kip/abstract
  "A numbered, cross-repository queue for changes to the normative surfaces of
   the kotoba-lang workspace, with states, an objection window that has a clock
   on it, and a quorum for admission. Fills the one gap between what EIP and
   IPIP do and what ADRs already do here: an ADR is written by the party that
   already decided, so nothing in this workspace can currently enumerate the
   changes that are pending."

  :kip/motivation
  "Measured 2026-08-16 across all 4,187 west projects: no repository, and no
   document inside kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang, describes a proposal process. What
   exists instead, and works, is:

   - ADRs, per repository. kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang has 43, the compiler has 221,
     the superproject has thousands. lang/surface-status.edn already says that
     widening an :intentional-security-constraint 'requires an ADR and
     fail-closed enforcement', so the ADR is already load-bearing.
   - docs/authority-map.edn, which names who owns each normative surface. This
     is the part EIP calls editors, and here it is machine-checked, which EIP's
     is not.
   - lang/version-policy.edn: a 180-day deprecation floor, removal-requires-major,
     release notes that must carry a surface-status diff, and signed release tags
     that bind the language profile. EIP has no equivalent.
   - kotoba-lang/kotoba-issue and kotoba-lang/kotoba-ledger: a working
     issue -> proposal -> review -> merge -> audit state machine with an
     append-only ledger. Its subject is agent effects, not specifications.
   - kagami govern: threshold signatures from an allow-list for canonical
     advances. Its subject is west pins, not specifications.

   So the organs exist and three of them are better than the thing being copied.
   What is missing is narrow and specific: there is no state between 'someone is
   thinking about it' and 'it is decided and written up', and therefore no way
   for a second person to object before the fact. An ADR records a decision. A
   KIP holds one open.

   The second missing thing follows from the first: because no queue exists,
   nothing can check that a change to a normative surface went through one."

  :kip/specification
  "### Tracks

   - standards      — changes a normative surface. Last call and quorum required.
   - process        — changes how this workspace decides. Editors close it.
   - informational  — a finding or guidance nothing must conform to.

   ### States

       draft -> review -> last-call -> final
                  |  ^        |  |
                  v  |        v  |
                draft |   review |
                  |   |          |
                  +---+----------+--> withdrawn (author) / rejected (editor)

       final -> superseded (quorum, requires :kip/superseded-by)

   last-call exists only on the standards track. Its minimum is 14 days,
   measured from :kip/last-call-started. The clock is the load-bearing part: a
   window with no minimum closes whenever the author is ready, which is the same
   as not having one. 14 days is the shortest interval that survives one person
   being away, which is the only failure mode a last call prevents.

   ### Roles

   :author is whoever wrote it. :editor is not a standing committee — for a
   given KIP it is the set of authority owners named by
   lang/normative-surfaces.edn for the surfaces that KIP touches. Who may
   advance a KIP is therefore a function of what it changes. :quorum is N
   distinct signers from the fleet governance allow-list, which is
   manifest/fleet-keys.edn :canonical :allow in the superproject — the same list
   kagami govern reads. That list is referenced, never copied: two copies of a
   key list is two answers to who may sign.

   ### Numbering

   kips/kip-NNNN.edn, four digits, monotonic, never reused. A withdrawn or
   rejected number stays spent, because reuse makes every external citation of
   'KIP-17' ambiguous, and being citable is what the number is for.

   ### Where the rules live

   lang/kip-process.edn is machine-normative and is what the validator reads.
   This document is the human-readable statement of the same process. If they
   disagree, the EDN wins and this document is the bug — the same split
   kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang keeps between docs/lang/semantics-ssot.md and
   lang/guest-grammar.edn.

   The judgement itself — may this KIP move from this state to that one — is a
   word-typed decision core in kotoba/kip_gate_core.kotoba, mirrored in
   src/kotoba/kip/core.cljk, with a parity test comparing every branch.

   ### What is deliberately not automated

   Signature verification is delegated. kotoba.kip.core/quorum-signers takes an
   optional :verify-fn; without one it counts claimed signers and reports
   :verified? false, and the gate prints that on its own line rather than
   folding an unchecked signature into a pass. Wiring it to kagami's ed25519
   verification is a later KIP, and until then no KIP can honestly reach :final.

   Coverage — did this change to a normative surface actually have a KIP — is
   not a fleet gate. Answering it needs another repository's git history and
   this registry at the same time, and a fleet gate is handed exactly one
   repository's tree. That check lives operator-side in the superproject at
   scripts/verify-kip-coverage.cljs, and it exits 2, distinct from both pass and
   fail, when it cannot see its inputs. The precedent is root-permit-index,
   which was landed as a fleet gate whose generator reads orgs/cloud-itonami —
   a path the root repository's git tree does not contain. It has run about 300
   times and has never been green."

